{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>The meal-offering of jealousy is in the hand of the priest, and they shall be.</b> I have already informed you in the section of the Dispersion that wherever you find the specific Name inverted, it is an allusion to the attribute of judgment, and in that matter the attribute of judgment is hinted as drawn taut. And I have already roused you above that they would write twenty-four words for the suspected woman that were erased upon the waters — which is by means of the Name of attribution, which is the attribute of judgment drawn taut against her." "<b>The bitter waters that cause curse.</b> They are called bitter on account of their end, and likewise (Job 24:6): ״ובגדי ערומים תפשיט״ — 'you strip the garment of the naked'; and likewise (Isaiah 47:2): ״קחי רחים וטחני קמח״ — 'take the millstones and grind flour.' And what it says, 'that cause curse' — on account of the curses erased into the waters, for they curse her." "<b>And the sage</b> R. Abraham, of blessed memory, mentioned in it a secret, and this is his language: the word מי is a noun and the word המרים is an epithet of the Name, but its secret is known — end quote. And I say that he spoke truly, for it is an epithet of the Name." "<b>And here I am</b> to explain its secret: for Scripture called the supernal powers מרים, for thus Job called them (Job 38:7?) — ״מרירי יום ואוררי יום״ — 'those who make bitter the day and those who curse the day.' This is what it says (Job 3:5): ״יבעתוהו כמרירי יום״ — 'let it terrify him like those who embitter the day'; and the כ is added, like the כ in (Hosea 5:10) ״כמסיגי גבול״ — 'like those who remove the boundary.' And it says (Job 3:8): ״יקבוהו אוררי יום״ — 'let those who curse the day cast their lot upon it' — and they are the supernal powers destined to rouse and crush the heads of Leviathan. And thus the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: Gabriel is destined to make sport with Leviathan, and were the Holy One, blessed be He, not to help him, he could not prevail against him, as it says (Job 40): ״העושו יגיש חרבו״." "<b>And consider</b> the language of ״מי המרים המאררים״ — for the מרים are those that curse her. And when the priest would adjure the woman by the honored Name, with the waters in his hand, he would direct his intention toward the two attributes — the attribute of day and the attribute of night — which concur to punish her if she had become defiled, or to grant her the reward of the fruit of the womb if she was innocent. And these two attributes are hinted to the discerning in the word מרים. And behold, they emanate and bestow power upon all the supernal powers called אוררי יום. Therefore he attributed the waters to the word מרים, saying ״המרים המאררים״ — that is, waters of the מרים that curse her. And from this the sage mentioned above said that it is an epithet and not a noun. And what it says, ״ובאו בה המים המאררים למרים״ — which appears to be a noun and not an epithet — this is its interpretation: ובאו בה המים המאררים שהם למרים — 'and the bitter waters that cause curse shall come into her, for they are למרים' — for the curses that are in the waters curse her by the power of the מרים, and the מרים by the power of the attribute of day and the attribute of night hinted in the section."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {3 "Hebrew text cites איוב מ׳:י״ט׳:י״׳ before the quotation, apparently a corrupt citation; the quoted phrase ״מרירי יום ואוררי יום״ does not match any extant verse. The phrase echoes Job 3:8 (אוררי יום). Retained the citation as printed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:00.907608035Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}